# 8. Localization operating model

## Global team

Owns:

- platform architecture;
- adaptive-learning science;
- security baseline;
- common analytics;
- core accessibility;
- experimentation standards;
- global claims policy;
- release management;
- model governance.

## Country team or accountable partner

Owns:

- curriculum crosswalks;
- local content commissioning;
- language QA;
- teacher and parent research;
- market-specific data review;
- procurement documentation;
- local training and support;
- partner performance;
- local incident escalation.

## Release authority

A country team may block release for:

- curriculum inaccuracies;
- harmful or culturally inappropriate content;
- language defects;
- unresolved privacy or child-safety risks;
- failure to meet contractual requirements.

The global team may block release for:

- security weaknesses;
- incompatible data structures;
- unsafe model behavior;
- invalid analytics;
- unreviewed use of learner data;
- changes that create unmaintainable market forks.

Every localization request should include:

- customer or regulatory problem;
- affected learners;
- expected learning or trust benefit;
- revenue opportunity;
- reuse potential;
- maintenance cost;
- proposed sunset or review date.

