# 9. Validation and scale gates

## 9.1 Discovery before build

For each market, conduct at least:

- 15–20 teacher interviews;
- 10–15 parent interviews;
- 5–10 school or tutoring leader interviews;
- 3–5 procurement or distribution interviews;
- classroom observation in different school types;
- language and content review by local subject specialists.

This work should produce a ranked list of must-have, should-have, and defer items. It should not become an open-ended localization project.

## 9.2 Product pilot

Run a 12–16 week pilot with:

- 8–12 institutions or organized cohorts;
- at least two school or customer types where relevant;
- 500–1,500 learners depending on market size;
- a documented implementation protocol;
- baseline diagnostic;
- teacher training;
- weekly technical monitoring;
- mid-pilot content review;
- endline learning assessment.

Measure separately:

### Product-market fit

- activation;
- weekly active learners;
- completion;
- teacher assignment and review;
- parent retention;
- support burden;
- willingness to pay;
- renewal or expansion intent.

### Implementation fidelity

- percentage of planned sessions delivered;
- teacher training completion;
- device and connectivity uptime;
- synchronization success;
- teacher time per class;
- use of recommended interventions;
- percentage of learners receiving the intended dosage.

### Learning efficacy

- pre/post mastery by local objective;
- performance on independently authored or externally reviewed assessments;
- progress relative to a comparison group or phased rollout;
- remediation success;
- performance by language, gender, school type, device, and connectivity.

Usage and satisfaction should never substitute for learning evidence.

## 9.3 Scale gates

A pilot may move to broader commercial release only if it achieves predetermined thresholds, for example:

- at least 80% of planned implementation sessions delivered;
- teacher setup and weekly administration time within an agreed limit;
- no unresolved critical safety or privacy defect;
- curriculum and language error rate below the release threshold;
- recommendation acceptance or override patterns showing no material systematic bias;
- statistically credible or practically meaningful learning improvement;
- support cost and infrastructure cost consistent with the business case;
- a credible renewal, partner, or procurement path.

A public-sector scale decision should require stronger evidence than a D2C release. D2C may proceed with validated usability and early learning evidence; ministry or donor expansion should generally require independent evaluation, implementation reliability, and clear value for money.

